<% content_for :title, "Home | Ruby on Rails Sample App" %>

<section class="bg-gradient-to-b from-rose-50 to-slate-50">
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-10 items-center">
    <div>
      <span class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-rose-700 bg-rose-100 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">CSC270 &mdash; Phase 1</span>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
        A modern web stack, the <span class="text-rose-700">Rails</span> way.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        Welcome to our Phase 1 proof-of-concept! Our team selected
        <strong>Ruby on Rails</strong> as our full-stack solution because it bundles
        a productive back-end framework, a powerful ORM (Active Record), and a clean
        view layer (ERB) into one cohesive platform. Pair that with Tailwind CSS for
        styling and SQLite for storage, and we have everything we need to ship a real
        feature in a single weekend.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        This sample app is intentionally simple: a few static pages, shared layout,
        navigation, and modern CSS. As we move into later phases we'll layer on
        models, forms, validations, authentication, and a real database &mdash; all
        without leaving the Rails ecosystem.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <%= link_to "Meet the team", about_path,
              class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-rose-700 hover:bg-rose-800 text-white px-5 py-2.5 rounded-md font-semibold shadow transition" %>
        <%= link_to "Contact us", contact_path,
              class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-white hover:bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 border border-slate-300 px-5 py-2.5 rounded-md font-semibold transition" %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-center">
      <%= image_tag "rails_hero.svg",
            alt: "Stylized Ruby on Rails logo",
            class: "w-full max-w-md drop-shadow-xl" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Why Ruby on Rails?</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-slate-700 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
    Rails is an opinionated, "convention over configuration" framework. The same tool
    that powers GitHub, Shopify, and Basecamp gives a small student team the
    productivity of a much larger one. Generators, scaffolding, and a thoughtful
    directory layout mean we spend our time on features, not boilerplate.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
    <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-6 shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition">
      <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-md bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 flex items-center justify-center font-bold">M</div>
      <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">MVC Architecture</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Models, views, and controllers live in well-defined folders so anyone on the
        team can find their way around on day one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-6 shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition">
      <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-md bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 flex items-center justify-center font-bold">A</div>
      <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Active Record ORM</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Talk to the database in Ruby instead of raw SQL, with migrations and
        validations built in.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-slate-200 p-6 shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition">
      <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-md bg-rose-100 text-rose-700 flex items-center justify-center font-bold">T</div>
      <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900">Tailwind CSS</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Utility-first CSS keeps the markup readable and the design consistent across
        every page in this sample app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
